#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161035dd-534e-5c12-88df-5fe26ee6a1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296142} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f91561-2994-50d4-8899-964b34007fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296143} {:answer/id #uuid "6870e8fd-8f88-5aed-8b88-d157b85205d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296144} {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} {:answer/id #uuid "966fc9a3-c000-5205-81d7-a858502c46c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b5e482-b9e5-5c06-9080-0da05e7a456c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "229d26fc-7842-5b85-86ac-4396d46bdbad" :question/math-academy-id "q-114045" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{16\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f31708-7364-5b00-bc40-83773c796def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296163} {:answer/id #uuid "c8be7c0f-6c6c-5515-aa19-d61eb40f1685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} {:answer/id #uuid "9320b7ee-1d53-5394-885b-36f361f9eed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296165} {:answer/id #uuid "0b740056-8d11-5c1e-837f-82dfab08f2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} {:answer/id #uuid "a2122e1b-e597-5789-a69c-dbb150b6008f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b740056-8d11-5c1e-837f-82dfab08f2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9358d7-9edc-56fd-b26d-a923c3cd4f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a2898ff-e121-5eb2-975f-1ad5f1f98f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-114084" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917cb601-d5eb-572d-bf4f-bb06a638fc75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287528} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e2fd85-f260-5896-b563-68fad839d972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287529} {:answer/id #uuid "6d26f927-4703-5484-9b89-b190a8cfc990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287530} {:answer/id #uuid "0d211053-2cb4-527f-9855-1c711ef74364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287531} {:answer/id #uuid "09012162-0dcf-5502-acf8-652fbf5f4664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(16x)}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1e2fd85-f260-5896-b563-68fad839d972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287529} :answer-field/id #uuid "2979d981-dd42-57b5-88bf-67d3e7add18c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2793cfa5-af9d-509f-b5fa-c5da2d7d9995" :question/math-academy-id "q-2504" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46957e03-babc-5006-941b-1dff564d17d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296198} {:answer/id #uuid "129371b6-76e9-5934-946e-e72a122be68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296199} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d58318-7ff7-57d8-95e8-e8ec001031d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(9x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296200} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae53925-8b35-560d-ab46-9848d465e7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296201} {:answer/id #uuid "b9090071-07b0-580b-b509-0f269fd31e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9090071-07b0-580b-b509-0f269fd31e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296202} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ecf06b-a040-58ea-a505-42586cff8955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e23bb3f4-a65e-57d7-abc7-33852f454847" :question/math-academy-id "q-49554" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $3x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
